WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.020
When it comes to comic book movies, it's important
to have a certain amount of suspension of

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:11.049
disbelief. Still, sometimes you can't help
but wonder exactly how some plot turns went

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:15.161
down. While Captain America: Civil War was
awesome from start to finish, there were

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:19.990
some moments that made us go, "Hey, wait,
a minute..." Oh, and watch out...major spoilers

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:20.590
ahead.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.490
Blurred Vision

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.660
The event that kick-starts the movie into
motion is the battle against Crossbones in

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.740
Lagos, Nigeria, which ends with the villain
attempting to explode Captain America and

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.050
himself to bits. He fails in his attempt,
thankfully, when Scarlet Witch shifts the

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.170
blast elsewhere. Unfortunately, her actions
accidentally lead to the deaths of several

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.480
relief aid workers. You know who probably
could've saved the day?

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.679
The Vision, who simply wasn't there. It's
not like he has an exciting personal life

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:51.399
or anything, so why was he absent? It's never
explained. But if we had to guess...it's because

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:55.170
his presence would've changed the nature of
the fight. And prevented that part of the

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.319
plot from working.

00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:57.819
No spin zone

00:00:57.819 --> 00:01:02.199
Before unveiling the Sokovia Accords, General
Ross takes the Avengers for a guilt trip down

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.700
memory lane. He reflects on the various incidents
that have taken place since their inception

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.600
as a team, focusing on the innocent casualties
and damages that happened as a result.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.439
The Battle of New York is one of his key talking
points—although he avoids mentioning how

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:20.340
the World Security Council ordered a nuclear
strike against the alien invasion in Manhattan,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.850
which certainly would've caused far more damage
than the Avengers did.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.750
Why didn't someone from the Avengers speak
up and point that out?

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.070
You'd also think that someone on the team
would've noted that Scarlet Witch's actions

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.969
in Lagos saved even more lives by removing
Crossbones' blast from ground level, which

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:36.399
was much more populated.

00:01:36.399 --> 00:01:40.890
In fact, pretty much all the incidents Ross
mentions would have been way worse without

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.580
the Avengers intervening, right? Shouldn't
one of the super-people mentioned that…?

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.580
OK, that's enough,

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:48.719
All about timing

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:52.549
Since the events of Avengers: Age of Ultron
that led to the deaths of his family, Civil

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:58.400
War bad guy Helmut Zemo plans to destroy
the Avengers by causing inner conflict, disintegrating

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:02.969
the group from within. What's confusing, though,
is that a lot of his plan seems dependent

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:08.090
on the Sokovia Accords. And they didn't even
come into play until well after his plan started,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:08.520
right?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.590
Let's just accept that he's fast on his feet
and adapted to the situation. Just like this

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:14.959
next situation...

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:17.540
What if Iron Man hadn't shown up?

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.050
So Zemo's big plan all along was to tear down
the Avengers by turning them against each

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.230
other right? When he shows Tony Stark the
video of his parents being slain by Bucky

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.929
while he was in Winter Soldier mode. Tempers flare

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:34.540
all around, and Cap's relationship with Iron
Man goes up in smoke. Hooray for Zemo! On

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.080
the other hand, what was Zemo planning to
do if Iron Man hadn't flown in when he did?

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.750
It's not like he had any reason to believe
that Iron Man was arriving: it was a spontaneous

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.069
decision.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:47.430
Of course, if there's one thing you can set
your watch to, it's Tony making crazy,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.659
disastrous, spontaneous decisions…

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:52.000
So maybe we'll give them partial credit on
that one.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.780
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
Channel to watch more videos like the one

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.050
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which plot holes we missed...

